WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.255 --> 00:00:07.632
What do you love about this country?

00:00:08.967 --> 00:00:13.680
I love that it's small, and
I love that we're existing,

00:00:15.015 --> 00:00:17.767
and we have to be proud
that we have a country.

00:01:03.855 --> 00:01:07.484
Kyrgyzstan aka the Kyrgyz Republic,

00:01:07.484 --> 00:01:10.570
is perhaps not the most
obvious place to visit

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:12.447
for a show about technology.

00:01:19.788 --> 00:01:22.749
Kyrgyzstan used to be
part of the Soviet Union,

00:01:22.749 --> 00:01:25.919
so it has lots of Soviet
stuff laying around.

00:01:27.045 --> 00:01:31.007
Soviet government
buildings, Soviet sculptures

00:01:31.966 --> 00:01:36.888
Soviet theme parks with an extra
dollop of wintery dystopia.

00:01:38.556 --> 00:01:40.934
There's even Vlad, the dead one,

00:01:40.934 --> 00:01:42.727
fresh from a snowball fight.

00:01:44.354 --> 00:01:46.606
In the winter, the capital city of Bishkek

00:01:46.606 --> 00:01:48.525
is, well, it's bleak.

00:01:49.359 --> 00:01:52.403
Coal and trash fires
envelope the city in smog.

00:01:53.488 --> 00:01:58.118
It's grim, then beautiful, then grim,

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:04.124
then beautiful all over
again, and also kind of weird.

00:02:07.502 --> 00:02:09.295
The city's circus, for example,

00:02:09.295 --> 00:02:12.006
takes place inside a decaying UFO.

00:02:16.678 --> 00:02:17.637
Now I'm not going to dazzle you

00:02:17.637 --> 00:02:20.557
with the usual robots and DNA editing.

00:02:22.684 --> 00:02:25.770
The story here is about
a fragile democracy

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:28.273
trying to find its place
in the modern world.

00:02:29.274 --> 00:02:33.361
Our people were so courageous
to go and do the revolution

00:02:33.361 --> 00:02:34.946
and fight for it.

00:02:34.946 --> 00:02:36.114
I saw it.

00:02:36.114 --> 00:02:38.324
They were shooting people.

00:02:38.324 --> 00:02:40.076
It's about a landlocked nation,

00:02:40.076 --> 00:02:42.537
one with very few natural resources,

00:02:42.537 --> 00:02:45.707
hoping to gen up a tech
industry on the fly.

00:02:45.707 --> 00:02:47.584
We are limited by resources.

00:02:47.584 --> 00:02:49.627
I like it that we don't
have the resources.

00:02:49.627 --> 00:02:52.714
Otherwise, if everything would
be okay, so we would be lazy.

00:02:55.675 --> 00:02:58.178
Kyrgyzstan, it turns
out, really wants to sell

00:02:58.178 --> 00:03:00.680
the rest of the world on
its software engineers

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:02.765
and its internet creators.

00:03:04.017 --> 00:03:07.562
And on that score, they've had
at least one major success.

00:03:07.562 --> 00:03:09.272
Believe it or not, some of the most

00:03:09.272 --> 00:03:12.108
popular videos on YouTube are cooked up

00:03:12.108 --> 00:03:15.111
right here in Bishkek.

00:03:15.111 --> 00:03:17.280
Hello world!

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:18.781
We are D Billions!

00:03:29.334 --> 00:03:31.628
Hello, welcome to D Billions home.

00:03:31.628 --> 00:03:33.463
Let me show you around.

00:03:33.463 --> 00:03:36.382
Here we make some shoots for our channel.

00:03:36.382 --> 00:03:38.384
This is the actors' room.

00:03:38.384 --> 00:03:39.594
Hello guys.

00:03:39.594 --> 00:03:42.680
Hello!
Hey!

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:47.018
Animation studio A, animation studio B.

00:03:47.852 --> 00:03:50.230
Welcome to audio recording studio.

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:51.439
What up, guys?

00:03:51.439 --> 00:03:52.273
Hello.

00:03:54.943 --> 00:03:58.238
I think all our singers are here.

00:04:00.865 --> 00:04:04.410
Aman, who got 2.5 billion views.

00:04:06.537 --> 00:04:07.956
Everybody knows these guys.

00:04:07.956 --> 00:04:10.750
Everybody knows these guys in Kyrgyzstan.

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:12.001
To be the part of this team,

00:04:12.001 --> 00:04:14.003
that's a dream team, really.

00:04:20.677 --> 00:04:24.806
I'm not going to lie, I
came here in small part

00:04:24.806 --> 00:04:27.267
to kind of make fun of D Billions.

00:04:28.101 --> 00:04:29.602
♪ Chicky cha-cha, boom-boom ♪

00:04:29.602 --> 00:04:31.688
♪ La, la, la, la, la ♪

00:04:31.688 --> 00:04:33.064
It's a kid show on YouTube,

00:04:33.064 --> 00:04:36.526
and if you have spawn,
there's a very good chance

00:04:36.526 --> 00:04:39.153
they have consumed some of this.

00:04:39.153 --> 00:04:42.490
♪ Mommy, mommy, mommy, mommy, mommy ♪

00:04:42.490 --> 00:04:44.158
It's not exactly high art.

00:04:44.158 --> 00:04:47.912
Some people might even call
it villainous ear poison.

00:04:47.912 --> 00:04:49.414
♪ Shake, shake move your body ♪

00:04:49.414 --> 00:04:52.500
♪ Shake, shake move your body ♪

00:04:55.378 --> 00:04:58.715
But whatever you think
doesn't really matter.

00:04:58.715 --> 00:05:03.720
They get the views, the billions
and the billions of them.

00:05:03.761 --> 00:05:05.972
This is our diamond button.

00:05:05.972 --> 00:05:07.890
First in Central Asia.

00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:09.892
This is our trophy.

00:05:09.892 --> 00:05:12.270
We achieve it that we got

00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:14.814
10 million.
Subscribers.

00:05:14.814 --> 00:05:17.108
How did a 50 person outfit

00:05:17.108 --> 00:05:19.444
in a country many people
have never heard of

00:05:19.444 --> 00:05:21.237
achieve this kind of success?

00:05:22.405 --> 00:05:24.907
To find out, I sat down with the founders.

00:05:24.907 --> 00:05:27.118
Husband and wife team,
Ernist and Cholponay.

00:05:28.661 --> 00:05:32.874
Ernist is a producer, and
Cholponay is a singer and actor.

00:05:32.874 --> 00:05:36.919
I do this for a living,
you know, research people.

00:05:36.919 --> 00:05:40.131
There's just, there's like
nothing about you guys.

00:05:40.131 --> 00:05:42.425
The Western press has not sort of

00:05:43.593 --> 00:05:45.511
It's going to be first interview.

00:05:45.511 --> 00:05:46.804
This one.
This.

00:05:46.804 --> 00:05:48.806
Right, okay, that's what I was wondering.

00:05:48.806 --> 00:05:50.058
Because we don't like it.

00:05:50.058 --> 00:05:52.185
We just want to work.

00:05:52.185 --> 00:05:56.064
For people who have never
seen any of your shows before,

00:05:56.064 --> 00:05:58.566
can you explain what D Billions is?

00:05:58.566 --> 00:06:03.571
D Billions is a group
focusing only on kids songs.

00:06:04.113 --> 00:06:09.035
We have 13 channels in
different languages.

00:06:09.035 --> 00:06:12.622
And the two of you, you're
a husband and wife, right?

00:06:12.622 --> 00:06:17.627
We grew up together studying
in theatre children's choir.

00:06:18.878 --> 00:06:20.588
That's called Ak Shoola.

00:06:23.549 --> 00:06:27.929
And we took part in many
international kids festivals,

00:06:27.929 --> 00:06:32.934
and when Ernist was 15 years
old, he created teenage group.

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:37.313
We were rapping, break dancing,

00:06:37.313 --> 00:06:40.233
singing classic songs, pop songs.

00:06:40.233 --> 00:06:42.860
That was the beginning of D Billions.

00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:47.865
We had all the experience we
needed to work on YouTube.

00:06:48.491 --> 00:06:51.786
We have four music videos
with billion views,

00:06:51.786 --> 00:06:56.791
and in addition, when we first
saw the "Baby Shark" video,

00:06:56.833 --> 00:06:58.960
it already had billion views.

00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:04.465
Ernist said, "I also
can make such a song."

00:07:06.050 --> 00:07:08.261
♪ Yummy, yummy, yummy, yummy, yummy ♪

00:07:08.261 --> 00:07:09.595
♪ So yummy ♪

00:07:09.595 --> 00:07:10.930
Here's how that went.

00:07:10.930 --> 00:07:13.349
♪ Yummy, yummy, so yummy ♪

00:07:13.349 --> 00:07:14.767
So what's the secret sauce?

00:07:14.767 --> 00:07:16.769
♪ I like it, I like it ♪

00:07:16.769 --> 00:07:21.691
I think kids loves the
rhythm of this song.

00:07:21.691 --> 00:07:23.609
Like, yummy, yummy, yummy, yummy.

00:07:24.652 --> 00:07:25.695
♪ So yummy ♪

00:07:25.695 --> 00:07:27.905
♪ Yummy, yummy, yummy, yummy ♪

00:07:27.905 --> 00:07:29.365
♪ So yummy ♪

00:07:29.365 --> 00:07:33.202
Kids loves to repeat it, and
there is one more secret.

00:07:33.202 --> 00:07:35.246
It's in minimalism.

00:07:35.246 --> 00:07:37.248
♪ I am a triangle ♪

00:07:37.248 --> 00:07:39.292
♪ I am a triangle ♪

00:07:39.292 --> 00:07:44.297
Every song has a goal to
teach one or two words

00:07:44.672 --> 00:07:47.800
without overloading kids' attention.

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:48.634
Unicorn.

00:07:49.802 --> 00:07:53.806
Statistics show that the
most minimalist songs

00:07:53.806 --> 00:07:56.434
break all records.

00:07:56.434 --> 00:07:58.644
You heard her, internet creators.

00:07:58.644 --> 00:08:01.355
Now go get those D Billions.

00:08:05.109 --> 00:08:09.655
Okay, so maybe it's not the
most mind improving content,

00:08:09.655 --> 00:08:12.033
but it is some kind of modern phenomenon.

00:08:13.284 --> 00:08:15.745
D Billions is just one of a dozen channels

00:08:15.745 --> 00:08:18.956
this company is feeding, and
their success has allowed them

00:08:18.956 --> 00:08:21.834
to recruit the best
singers, graphic artists,

00:08:21.834 --> 00:08:23.794
and composers in the country

00:08:23.794 --> 00:08:26.506
all to pump out videos at hyper speed.

00:08:27.340 --> 00:08:30.676
This is a very American, crass question.

00:08:30.676 --> 00:08:34.138
How big is the business in
terms of not just viewers,

00:08:34.138 --> 00:08:37.266
but it must be like huge
in terms of revenue?

00:08:37.558 --> 00:08:43.064
Perhaps because we are a Kyrgyz company,

00:08:43.064 --> 00:08:47.985
we are on the list of high earning companies in Kyrgyzstan.

00:08:47.985 --> 00:08:53.199
But if you compare our revenue with American companies,

00:08:53.199 --> 00:08:56.953
we would be one of the smallest.

00:08:57.495 --> 00:09:01.123
Ernist didn't want to
get into exact numbers.

00:09:01.123 --> 00:09:04.752
So let's take a wild guess, shall we?

00:09:05.753 --> 00:09:08.548
Other YouTube kids channels
with similar view counts

00:09:08.548 --> 00:09:10.174
have reported earning millions,

00:09:10.174 --> 00:09:12.593
even tens of millions of dollars per year.

00:09:13.469 --> 00:09:14.887
D Billions might take a hit

00:09:14.887 --> 00:09:17.598
from having many viewers outside the US,

00:09:17.598 --> 00:09:20.851
but it's safe to say they're
doing well for themselves.

00:09:21.686 --> 00:09:23.104
The whole enterprise gets a boost

00:09:23.104 --> 00:09:25.648
from the Kyrgyz government
and industry too

00:09:25.648 --> 00:09:28.317
through a programme called IT Park.

00:09:28.317 --> 00:09:31.112
The hope is to conjure a
flourishing Kyrgyz tech industry

00:09:31.112 --> 00:09:34.949
out of thin air by giving
companies like D Billions

00:09:34.949 --> 00:09:36.867
massive tax breaks.

00:09:36.867 --> 00:09:39.579
Participants of IT Park, they are free

00:09:39.579 --> 00:09:44.333
from all taxes except 1% income tax,

00:09:44.333 --> 00:09:46.586
and we are participants of IT Park,

00:09:46.586 --> 00:09:51.299
and that's been a great
support from our government.

00:09:55.845 --> 00:09:59.640
But the picture wasn't always
this rosy for D Billions.

00:09:59.640 --> 00:10:02.101
Virality might come easy to these folks,

00:10:02.101 --> 00:10:04.186
but trying to get paid on YouTube,

00:10:04.186 --> 00:10:06.397
that's a whole other thing.

00:10:06.397 --> 00:10:09.984
Before the D Billions, there
was a channel AWA, A-W-A.

00:10:09.984 --> 00:10:14.989
We were working there, and
we got one hit cartoon.

00:10:15.364 --> 00:10:18.659
It was so 42 million views.

00:10:18.659 --> 00:10:21.037
That was for us, phenomenal.

00:10:21.037 --> 00:10:23.080
I would kill for 42 million views

00:10:23.080 --> 00:10:25.291
42 million views, oh my God.

00:10:25.291 --> 00:10:26.751
That was unbelievable.

00:10:26.751 --> 00:10:30.171
That moment was for us like phenomenal.

00:10:30.171 --> 00:10:33.215
They said like, "Wow, we sold 42 million."

00:10:34.216 --> 00:10:35.384
But then

00:10:35.384 --> 00:10:40.056
Then they cut all the
monetization of A-W-A.

00:10:40.890 --> 00:10:42.433
And we didn't get any

00:10:42.433 --> 00:10:44.393
money from it.
YouTube cut the money?

00:10:44.393 --> 00:10:46.020
Yes. Yes.
YouTube cut the money?

00:10:46.020 --> 00:10:46.854
Why?

00:10:46.854 --> 00:10:47.688
Demonetization.
Why?

00:10:47.688 --> 00:10:48.981
We break some terms.

00:10:48.981 --> 00:10:50.733
We didn't know some rules.

00:10:50.733 --> 00:10:51.984
That's why.

00:10:51.984 --> 00:10:54.570
And nobody would tell us all the rules

00:10:55.488 --> 00:10:57.239
what we should to do.

00:10:59.617 --> 00:11:02.328
We were working two years just for free.

00:11:02.328 --> 00:11:04.747
Yeah, yeah.
It was very hard time.

00:11:04.747 --> 00:11:06.332
We were starving.

00:11:06.332 --> 00:11:07.166
So it was it sounds like

00:11:07.166 --> 00:11:09.835
it was sort of frightening, like, scary.

00:11:09.835 --> 00:11:11.003
Yeah, it was.

00:11:11.003 --> 00:11:15.257
So you were a couple with a young child,

00:11:15.257 --> 00:11:17.718
and taking all this financial risk.

00:11:17.718 --> 00:11:19.428
Money, no.

00:11:21.263 --> 00:11:23.599
Belief. Talk of belief.

00:11:34.652 --> 00:11:36.362
I may have come here to tease,

00:11:36.362 --> 00:11:39.240
but my time with the D
Billions crew left me amazed.

00:11:40.783 --> 00:11:43.160
The vibe at the office
was more hardworking,

00:11:43.160 --> 00:11:47.623
fun-loving family than more
unfilled influencer mansion,

00:11:47.623 --> 00:11:49.875
and there was creativity everywhere.

00:11:57.508 --> 00:11:59.677
Boom-Boom can play
basically any instrument.

00:12:02.763 --> 00:12:06.058
Ernist and Cholponay have
an obvious eye for talent,

00:12:06.058 --> 00:12:08.477
and a gift for reading
the whims of the internet.

00:12:13.065 --> 00:12:14.942
And instead of staff meetings,

00:12:14.942 --> 00:12:18.404
D Billions has all hands
campfire sing-alongs.

00:12:27.872 --> 00:12:30.124
This was just a small warm-up

00:12:30.124 --> 00:12:32.418
before shooting our cartoons.

00:12:34.837 --> 00:12:37.214
Of course, to really
understand D Billions,

00:12:38.924 --> 00:12:41.010
I had to become D Billions.

00:12:48.601 --> 00:12:51.312
Behold, American Daddy.

00:12:52.730 --> 00:12:55.024
Saviour of YouTube children.

00:13:03.115 --> 00:13:06.827
I'm about to be a superhero
in a D Billions video.

00:13:06.827 --> 00:13:09.205
Go to Kyrgyzstan, and
amazing things happen.

00:13:10.956 --> 00:13:14.126
When you do it at the end,

00:13:14.126 --> 00:13:16.837
just you can do like flying up.

00:13:16.837 --> 00:13:18.714
Okay.
Same, but with this side.

00:13:18.714 --> 00:13:19.632
Okay.

00:13:19.632 --> 00:13:23.552
I'm super dad, people are in trouble.

00:13:23.552 --> 00:13:25.137
I must save them.

00:13:25.137 --> 00:13:26.305
You ready?
Yeah.

00:13:26.305 --> 00:13:27.765
Camera. Action.

00:13:29.183 --> 00:13:30.768
Help, help, help!

00:13:31.936 --> 00:13:33.354
Where are they?

00:13:39.151 --> 00:13:42.071
It's okay, now we're on the ground.

00:13:43.364 --> 00:13:45.533
Don't worry, don't worry.

00:14:00.631 --> 00:14:02.925
Thank you for teaching
me to be a superhero.

00:14:04.927 --> 00:14:07.680
Now I want to see the professionals work.

00:14:18.357 --> 00:14:20.526
D Billions told me they would
animate this all together

00:14:20.526 --> 00:14:22.611
into something amazing.

00:14:27.575 --> 00:14:29.493
In the meantime, allow me to tell you

00:14:29.493 --> 00:14:33.873
some less kid-friendly things
about the Kyrgyz Republic.

00:14:39.670 --> 00:14:43.757
The country became part of
the Soviet Union in 1936.

00:14:43.757 --> 00:14:46.385
The Soviets did their best to try and turn

00:14:46.385 --> 00:14:50.306
a rural, very poor society
into something else.

00:14:50.306 --> 00:14:52.683
Mostly, they poured money into Bishkek,

00:14:52.683 --> 00:14:54.894
and the buildings and people followed.

00:14:56.186 --> 00:14:59.523
The Kyrgyz Republic arrived in 1991.

00:14:59.523 --> 00:15:03.944
It's a democracy, but not
exactly a grade A democracy.

00:15:03.944 --> 00:15:06.655
Votes can be bought and sold,

00:15:06.655 --> 00:15:09.658
and there's a not
infrequent number of coups.

00:15:10.826 --> 00:15:13.412
It's a Muslim nation, but
religion does not feel

00:15:13.412 --> 00:15:15.039
like it takes centre stage here.

00:15:16.290 --> 00:15:19.168
Today, the country's ties
to Russia remain strong

00:15:19.168 --> 00:15:24.048
with cheap energy meant to
keep the Kyrgyz people sweet.

00:15:24.048 --> 00:15:25.507
It's a precarious position,

00:15:25.507 --> 00:15:29.178
and one from which many people
here hope to break free.

00:15:30.346 --> 00:15:32.014
D Billions is meant to represent

00:15:32.014 --> 00:15:34.975
what Kyrgyzstan can be in the future.

00:15:34.975 --> 00:15:39.021
A more modern nation, one full
of creativity and expression.

00:15:43.525 --> 00:15:45.945
But this journey is not always smooth.

00:15:56.497 --> 00:15:59.416
Take for example the story of Zere.

00:16:02.461 --> 00:16:04.797
One of the most famous
singers in the country.

00:16:09.134 --> 00:16:12.638
She's YouTube famous, too,
but in a very different way.

00:16:14.515 --> 00:16:17.768
I started thinking, okay,
I should do something big,

00:16:17.768 --> 00:16:19.436
something about women's rights

00:16:19.436 --> 00:16:22.690
because that's what I care
about deeply right now.

00:16:29.488 --> 00:16:31.740
And so I just started writing a song,

00:16:33.450 --> 00:16:36.120
I made my friends listen to it.

00:16:36.120 --> 00:16:38.038
They said maybe I should do a video,

00:16:38.038 --> 00:16:40.582
so that's how it all started.

00:16:40.582 --> 00:16:42.001
And you did go viral.

00:16:42.001 --> 00:16:44.586
I did, I guess. Yeah.

00:16:51.176 --> 00:16:53.762
When you did this song,
obviously the lyrics of the song

00:16:53.762 --> 00:16:57.057
brought up a lot of issues
that some people were,

00:16:57.057 --> 00:17:00.686
you know, happy, very happy
to see being discussed openly,

00:17:00.686 --> 00:17:02.688
and obviously a lot of
people were less happy.

00:17:02.688 --> 00:17:05.691
And then in the video, I
think you were wearing a bra

00:17:05.691 --> 00:17:07.443
and people were, you know, what happened?

00:17:07.443 --> 00:17:12.406
So the reaction was basically enormous.

00:17:12.573 --> 00:17:15.451
I thought that the provoking stuff

00:17:15.451 --> 00:17:19.371
would be seeing like a girl in hijab

00:17:19.371 --> 00:17:22.374
besides a girl in a bikini, and, you know,

00:17:22.374 --> 00:17:26.587
all of that diversity that
has never been seen before.

00:17:26.587 --> 00:17:28.922
But the provoking thing was something

00:17:28.922 --> 00:17:32.134
that I literally just
decided like, in a second.

00:17:32.134 --> 00:17:34.011
You know, I was like, "Okay,
I'm going to look like this."

00:17:34.011 --> 00:17:39.016
That was the main trigger for
the whole dialogue to start.

00:17:39.767 --> 00:17:43.020
I was sort of ready for some backlash,

00:17:43.020 --> 00:17:45.564
sort of ready for some, you know,

00:17:45.564 --> 00:17:47.191
bullying or stuff like that,

00:17:47.191 --> 00:17:49.902
but it always seems easy in theory.

00:17:49.902 --> 00:17:52.071
You got death threats.

00:17:52.071 --> 00:17:54.364
I did, I did get death threats,

00:17:54.364 --> 00:17:57.326
and there were a lot of them actually.

00:17:57.326 --> 00:17:59.787
Do you still face a lot of the pushback,

00:17:59.787 --> 00:18:01.413
or has it sort of died down?

00:18:01.413 --> 00:18:05.042
Like, is it uncomfortable for
you to walk down the street?

00:18:05.042 --> 00:18:08.504
I wouldn't say I'm comfortable
walking in any street.

00:18:08.504 --> 00:18:09.671
It was a terrible example.

00:18:09.671 --> 00:18:12.007
Yeah, 'cause I've heard
you sing about this, yeah.

00:18:12.007 --> 00:18:15.094
Yeah, like, on top of the cat calling

00:18:15.094 --> 00:18:17.554
and everything that you get as a woman,

00:18:17.554 --> 00:18:20.015
if you're also like a feminist

00:18:20.015 --> 00:18:22.810
who was naked on the
screen and all of that,

00:18:22.810 --> 00:18:25.562
that also puts other layers, you know?

00:18:30.442 --> 00:18:32.903
Part of the reaction was
how you were dressed.

00:18:32.903 --> 00:18:35.989
Part of the reaction was the
things you were talking about,

00:18:35.989 --> 00:18:37.533
and the stories I read just said

00:18:37.533 --> 00:18:40.410
these are things that
aren't often discussed.

00:18:40.410 --> 00:18:44.623
Well, I think they were always discussed,

00:18:44.623 --> 00:18:47.876
not just collectively like that, you know?

00:18:47.876 --> 00:18:52.881
Not through one thing that
everyone has an opinion on.

00:18:53.048 --> 00:18:58.053
How would you describe the
issues that women face broadly?

00:18:58.345 --> 00:19:03.183
For an average woman in
Kyrgyzstan, it's hard everywhere.

00:19:05.060 --> 00:19:08.147
Like, at home, in the streets,

00:19:08.147 --> 00:19:12.317
you can literally get kidnapped,
like, and made a bride.

00:19:12.317 --> 00:19:15.821
You're supposed to marry
your rapist basically

00:19:15.821 --> 00:19:18.282
when you get kidnapped as a bride.

00:19:18.282 --> 00:19:20.284
Unfortunately, it still happens.

00:19:20.284 --> 00:19:25.289
It doesn't happen as often
as, you know, before,

00:19:25.289 --> 00:19:26.582
but still, it does happen.

00:19:34.590 --> 00:19:38.177
Kyrgyzstan is bizarrely
famous, or rather infamous,

00:19:38.177 --> 00:19:40.888
for the practise of bride kidnapping.

00:19:40.888 --> 00:19:44.808
Women are grabbed off the
streets and forced into marriage.

00:19:44.808 --> 00:19:46.894
No one in the country seems to agree

00:19:46.894 --> 00:19:48.770
on how prevalent this is.

00:19:48.770 --> 00:19:50.480
Although in my short time here,

00:19:50.480 --> 00:19:53.609
I met several women who told
me they had been kidnapped,

00:19:53.609 --> 00:19:56.612
or had family members
who had been kidnapped.

00:19:56.612 --> 00:19:59.865
The practise was only made
illegal a couple of years ago,

00:19:59.865 --> 00:20:03.744
and it's questionable how much
the law is actually enforced.

00:20:03.744 --> 00:20:07.998
If a woman gets harassed,
beaten up at home,

00:20:07.998 --> 00:20:11.126
assaulted in the streets,
and a lot of other stuff

00:20:11.126 --> 00:20:14.254
that can happen to women,
you go to the police,

00:20:14.254 --> 00:20:16.381
police is never going to help you.

00:20:16.381 --> 00:20:19.468
I've never seen one case in my life

00:20:19.468 --> 00:20:23.680
where the police was actually
helpful in these issues.

00:20:23.680 --> 00:20:25.098
We asked the Kyrgyz government

00:20:25.098 --> 00:20:29.478
to comment on all this,
but we never heard back.

00:20:29.478 --> 00:20:34.233
And can I just add that I'm
saying this absolutely like,

00:20:34.233 --> 00:20:36.944
being in love with this
country, you know what I mean?

00:20:36.944 --> 00:20:40.656
Like, it's not like I am
complaining, and like,

00:20:40.656 --> 00:20:41.865
trying to go somewhere.

00:20:41.865 --> 00:20:44.993
No, like, I want to be among those people

00:20:44.993 --> 00:20:48.830
who try and make Kyrgyzstan at least like,

00:20:48.830 --> 00:20:50.999
a safer place to live.

00:21:02.761 --> 00:21:05.013
Is there a sense that things are changing?

00:21:05.013 --> 00:21:08.058
If I look, there was a female
president relatively recently.

00:21:08.058 --> 00:21:10.435
I think the first in Central Asia.

00:21:10.435 --> 00:21:12.479
Is there a sense that
things are changing or like,

00:21:12.479 --> 00:21:15.983
do you feel like it's just
the same as it ever was?

00:21:15.983 --> 00:21:20.904
I myself, I'm very amazed and inspired

00:21:20.904 --> 00:21:23.699
by a lot of what women do in our country.

00:21:30.956 --> 00:21:33.667
And you're right, like
relatively speaking,

00:21:33.667 --> 00:21:36.420
in the region where we are,

00:21:36.420 --> 00:21:39.715
we have some better stuff going on,

00:21:39.715 --> 00:21:42.592
but that's relatively speaking, you know?

00:21:46.680 --> 00:21:49.182
There's a lot still to do for sure.

00:22:00.694 --> 00:22:02.279
The next night, the D Billions crew

00:22:02.279 --> 00:22:06.074
invites me out for an evening
of revelry, Bishkek style.

00:22:11.038 --> 00:22:13.999
♪ Everything's going to be all right ♪

00:22:13.999 --> 00:22:17.377
♪ Everything's going to be all right ♪

00:22:26.762 --> 00:22:29.389
After getting good and
tipsy on Kyrgyz lager,

00:22:31.350 --> 00:22:34.102
it's time for the grand unveiling.

00:22:34.102 --> 00:22:36.396
This is as exciting as it gets.

00:22:36.396 --> 00:22:41.401
I get to see myself as USA
Super Daddy on a big screen.

00:23:22.317 --> 00:23:24.194
There's every chance D
Billions is a one-off

00:23:24.194 --> 00:23:27.739
that got lucky hitting
the internet just right.

00:23:33.995 --> 00:23:37.541
But Kyrgyzstan is betting and
hoping that this is the start

00:23:37.541 --> 00:23:41.044
of a full on tech revolution
within the country.

00:24:02.566 --> 00:24:05.152
The woman riding this
horse so majestically

00:24:05.152 --> 00:24:06.027
is Tavita.

00:24:09.322 --> 00:24:11.992
And she symbolizes a lot about Kyrgyzstan.

00:24:13.618 --> 00:24:16.037
This is a place where the
people take great pride

00:24:16.037 --> 00:24:17.956
in the natural beauty of the country.

00:24:19.458 --> 00:24:23.211
It's also a place fond of its
traditions, in nomadic past.

00:24:25.755 --> 00:24:26.590
Tavita though,

00:24:26.590 --> 00:24:28.842
is also part of something
new and different here.

00:24:30.844 --> 00:24:33.763
When not galloping, she's coding.

00:24:39.519 --> 00:24:42.939
She is part of a wave of
thousands of young Kyrgyz students

00:24:42.939 --> 00:24:45.358
being trained as software engineers.

00:24:48.111 --> 00:24:51.323
The hope is to create a
homegrown Kyrgyz tech industry,

00:24:51.323 --> 00:24:53.617
which can serve as an economic engine

00:24:53.617 --> 00:24:55.368
to power the country's future.

00:24:59.206 --> 00:25:01.958
Naturally, I went to a fine-dining yurt

00:25:01.958 --> 00:25:04.336
with Tavita and her sister Avigail,

00:25:04.336 --> 00:25:06.004
who also works in tech

00:25:06.004 --> 00:25:08.423
to learn more about their experiences.

00:25:11.092 --> 00:25:12.219
- What do we have?

00:25:12.219 --> 00:25:17.140
- So different kinds of bread and borsok.

00:25:17.140 --> 00:25:18.558
It's like fried dough.

00:25:18.558 --> 00:25:19.643
- [Ashlee] Smells good.

00:25:19.643 --> 00:25:22.187
- Yeah, these are different types of meat.

00:25:22.187 --> 00:25:23.813
And horse meat as well.

00:25:23.813 --> 00:25:27.150
- Okay. How did the two of
you get into technology?

00:25:27.150 --> 00:25:30.111
'Cause you both have interest, obviously.

00:25:30.111 --> 00:25:34.407
- So my sister first started
interested in IT sphere

00:25:34.407 --> 00:25:37.702
And after that she said she recommended me

00:25:40.705 --> 00:25:41.540
to get into the IT industry.

00:25:41.540 --> 00:25:42.374
- What have you been doing?

00:25:42.374 --> 00:25:43.959
- Frontend development.
- Okay.

00:25:43.959 --> 00:25:45.919
I'm the middle of front-end developer.

00:25:45.919 --> 00:25:46.753
- What kind of company?

00:25:46.753 --> 00:25:49.047
Meanwhile, Tavita's
sister Avigail has a job

00:25:49.047 --> 00:25:52.342
telling the world about
Kyrgyzstan's coding efforts.

00:25:52.342 --> 00:25:54.469
- You want to work with
American tech communities,

00:25:54.469 --> 00:25:58.181
businesses, you have to
provide really high quality.

00:25:58.181 --> 00:26:00.559
So our pitch was that we've been working,

00:26:00.559 --> 00:26:01.643
we are experienced,

00:26:01.643 --> 00:26:04.813
we already have clients and
also cheap infrastructure,

00:26:04.813 --> 00:26:08.108
like electricity and also demographics.

00:26:08.108 --> 00:26:10.652
Very young people, there
are a lot of talents.

00:26:10.652 --> 00:26:13.196
And I think that this is because

00:26:13.196 --> 00:26:17.075
there was like huge focus on mathematics.

00:26:17.075 --> 00:26:19.911
And people are being just very motivated.

00:26:21.121 --> 00:26:22.789
- It's a pretty convincing story,

00:26:22.789 --> 00:26:24.958
which is good because the stakes are high.

00:26:28.128 --> 00:26:31.381
Kyrgyzstan has a nearly 30% poverty rate.

00:26:31.381 --> 00:26:34.884
And a per capita GDP
only a mother could love.

00:26:36.052 --> 00:26:38.138
With few natural resources to mine,

00:26:38.138 --> 00:26:40.849
and little in the way of big industry,

00:26:40.849 --> 00:26:42.767
it needs to look outside.

00:26:42.767 --> 00:26:44.811
The goal for the country is
to have how many programmers?

00:26:44.811 --> 00:26:48.440
- 50,000.
- 50,000, by?

00:26:48.440 --> 00:26:49.983
- 2030.
- By 2030.

00:26:49.983 --> 00:26:50.817
- Yeah.

00:26:50.817 --> 00:26:53.028
- And what number are we at today?

00:26:53.028 --> 00:26:54.613
- I think we're around 7,000.

00:26:54.613 --> 00:26:55.488
- 7,000. All right.

00:26:55.488 --> 00:26:57.657
So you're part of like the 7,000 then?

00:26:57.657 --> 00:26:58.491
- Yeah.

00:27:01.995 --> 00:27:05.206
- To build a 50,000-strong software army,

00:27:05.206 --> 00:27:06.583
you need boot camps.

00:27:10.754 --> 00:27:12.881
This is the headquarters of Codify,

00:27:12.881 --> 00:27:16.134
one of several companies in
Bishkek with coding programs

00:27:16.134 --> 00:27:18.595
for people of all ages.

00:27:18.595 --> 00:27:20.805
It was started by Dinara Ruslan.

00:27:20.805 --> 00:27:23.308
She grew up here, moved to Silicon Valley,

00:27:23.308 --> 00:27:25.060
and then came back with the hopes

00:27:25.060 --> 00:27:28.271
of building a tech giant in Bishkek.

00:27:28.271 --> 00:27:31.524
- I was doing IT project
product management

00:27:31.524 --> 00:27:33.777
and I always needed project managers.

00:27:34.653 --> 00:27:35.570
But I couldn't get them

00:27:35.570 --> 00:27:39.074
because no one actually prepares
them here in Central Asia.

00:27:39.074 --> 00:27:42.077
So then I started teaching it myself.

00:27:42.077 --> 00:27:44.204
- You have this fascinating background

00:27:44.204 --> 00:27:47.415
because you worked in Silicon
Valley and technology,

00:27:47.415 --> 00:27:50.085
I saw you worked in Iowa in technology.

00:27:50.085 --> 00:27:52.337
And I mean, for you to
come back and do this,

00:27:52.337 --> 00:27:54.714
you must have some hope that you can help

00:27:54.714 --> 00:27:57.300
in this push in Kyrgyzstan
to sort of modernize.

00:27:57.300 --> 00:28:00.011
Or is this, you just saw
a business opportunity?

00:28:00.970 --> 00:28:02.722
- Like when you grow up in a family

00:28:02.722 --> 00:28:06.851
where you like low
income, low-class family,

00:28:06.851 --> 00:28:08.728
you just don't think on like,

00:28:08.728 --> 00:28:10.313
"Oh my God, I'm going to help my country."

00:28:10.313 --> 00:28:11.147
No, you think like,

00:28:11.147 --> 00:28:13.525
"Oh my god, what I'm
going to eat tomorrow?"

00:28:13.525 --> 00:28:15.443
I'm going to survive, you know?

00:28:15.443 --> 00:28:18.238
So I'm going to lie if I say,

00:28:18.238 --> 00:28:20.573
"Oh, I want to help the country, whatever.

00:28:20.573 --> 00:28:24.119
No, I grow up thinking that
I have to become someone."

00:28:27.122 --> 00:28:28.415
- Dinara did become someone.

00:28:28.415 --> 00:28:31.126
A Product Manager at Wells Fargo.

00:28:31.126 --> 00:28:33.086
And tasted the decadent delights

00:28:33.086 --> 00:28:36.798
that the magical land of
California has to offer.

00:28:36.798 --> 00:28:40.343
- You get a job and you are making money.

00:28:40.343 --> 00:28:42.887
And you start going to mall on weekends,

00:28:42.887 --> 00:28:45.056
and then to cinema on
Fridays with your friends.

00:28:45.056 --> 00:28:46.015
And then you think,

00:28:46.015 --> 00:28:49.144
"Oh my God, this is the life,
this is the American dream."

00:28:49.144 --> 00:28:51.730
I was taking Uber and then I was buying

00:28:51.730 --> 00:28:55.275
and ordering this weekly vegetables

00:28:55.275 --> 00:28:57.569
to be delivered to my house.

00:28:57.569 --> 00:29:02.240
But then, it snapped. It was May, 2016.

00:29:02.240 --> 00:29:03.575
I was thinking,

00:29:03.575 --> 00:29:08.580
"I haven't seen my family and
my country in four years."

00:29:09.080 --> 00:29:11.416
I just decided, took a vacation,

00:29:11.416 --> 00:29:13.877
and then I was supposed
to leave to go back to my,

00:29:13.877 --> 00:29:16.254
another dream job in California.

00:29:16.254 --> 00:29:20.133
And then I got this job
offer from local startup.

00:29:24.596 --> 00:29:25.847
- Dinara never left.

00:29:25.847 --> 00:29:29.058
And eventually started
several companies of her own.

00:29:29.058 --> 00:29:32.395
She might frame her decision
to stay as strictly business.

00:29:32.395 --> 00:29:34.731
But her coding school is very much part

00:29:34.731 --> 00:29:36.316
of the national project.

00:29:36.316 --> 00:29:38.276
Preparing little ones to participate

00:29:38.276 --> 00:29:40.445
in the economy of the future.

00:29:41.196 --> 00:29:43.823
My name is Aybike, I’m seven years old

00:29:43.823 --> 00:29:46.451
and I’m studying Scratch.

00:29:46.659 --> 00:29:50.955
- My name is Lea, I'm
15 and I'm study HTML.

00:29:50.955 --> 00:29:53.333
- My name is Zoldana. I'm 16 years old.

00:29:53.333 --> 00:29:55.126
And I'm studying here JavaScript.

00:29:56.085 --> 00:29:58.087
- And Dinara takes particular pride

00:29:58.087 --> 00:30:01.007
in helping young women become tech-savvy.

00:30:01.007 --> 00:30:03.426
- If you look at the IT scene,

00:30:03.426 --> 00:30:07.138
if you even look at my
office, 80% is the girls.

00:30:07.138 --> 00:30:09.015
You're going to see
other startup founders.

00:30:09.015 --> 00:30:10.725
They're also like female founders.

00:30:10.725 --> 00:30:13.645
So you'll see that the
Kyrgyzstan is different.

00:30:18.983 --> 00:30:20.026
- It's a happy fact

00:30:20.026 --> 00:30:23.571
that women have a strong presence
in the Kyrgyz tech scene.

00:30:23.571 --> 00:30:26.115
I met a good number of
female leaders and engineers

00:30:26.115 --> 00:30:28.284
during my visit to Bishkek.

00:30:28.284 --> 00:30:29.536
They're not hard to find.

00:30:31.579 --> 00:30:34.165
But the picture is complicated.

00:30:34.165 --> 00:30:37.502
Kyrgyz society can be
brutally chauvinistic.

00:30:37.502 --> 00:30:40.213
Women are often expected to
take on traditional roles

00:30:40.213 --> 00:30:44.634
in the home, not to learn
to code, or pursue a career.

00:30:44.634 --> 00:30:48.680
- Society's opinion will affect
you if you think about it.

00:30:48.680 --> 00:30:50.181
If you don't think about it,

00:30:50.181 --> 00:30:52.684
then just study and do your job.

00:30:52.684 --> 00:30:55.395
Like who cares what that person is saying,

00:30:55.395 --> 00:30:56.563
that you can or cannot do?

00:30:56.563 --> 00:30:57.772
If you think,
- Yeah.

00:30:57.772 --> 00:30:59.649
- If you believe that
you can do something,

00:30:59.649 --> 00:31:01.985
that's all that matters.

00:31:01.985 --> 00:31:03.862
- When it comes to women's issues,

00:31:03.862 --> 00:31:06.406
Dinara is quick to accentuate the positive

00:31:06.406 --> 00:31:08.783
and insist that anyone can make it.

00:31:12.203 --> 00:31:15.081
But that's no surprise
coming from a self-made woman

00:31:15.081 --> 00:31:17.625
who founded three
companies by the age of 30.

00:31:18.710 --> 00:31:22.505
If anyone can bootstrap
Kyrgyzstan into tech prosperity,

00:31:22.505 --> 00:31:23.965
it's people like her.

00:31:23.965 --> 00:31:27.802
- Do we have a future?
Yes. Is it bright one?

00:31:27.802 --> 00:31:31.931
I think yes, because I'm a dreamer.

00:31:31.931 --> 00:31:36.185
I actually have a dream
to build a first unicorn

00:31:36.185 --> 00:31:39.856
out of Kyrgyzstan and Central Asia.

00:31:39.856 --> 00:31:42.025
Hopefully Codify.

00:31:42.025 --> 00:31:44.402
- I for one, wouldn't bet against her.

00:32:06.424 --> 00:32:09.177
"Don't go to Kyrgyzstan in
the winter," they told me.

00:32:11.512 --> 00:32:13.014
"It's gray and polluted.

00:32:14.599 --> 00:32:16.809
It's a horrible time to visit," they said.

00:32:22.774 --> 00:32:27.195
- But then, how else
would I have seen this?

00:32:33.618 --> 00:32:35.370
It's Alaman Ulak time,

00:32:35.370 --> 00:32:38.539
in a Kyrgyz village about
three hours from Bishkek.

00:32:39.916 --> 00:32:43.628
You get a goat, the goat suffers.

00:32:46.756 --> 00:32:49.050
And then you have what amounts to a ball.

00:32:55.431 --> 00:32:58.893
And whoever has the ball is under attack.

00:33:04.482 --> 00:33:06.776
The goal on this day
is to get the goat back

00:33:06.776 --> 00:33:08.152
to your village first.

00:33:10.822 --> 00:33:12.532
Then you get to eat the goat.

00:33:15.994 --> 00:33:18.913
And it's been well tenderized.

00:33:18.913 --> 00:33:19.747
Whoa!

00:33:34.512 --> 00:33:38.099
Clearly this is a place that
keeps its traditions alive.

00:33:39.642 --> 00:33:41.602
At least the super epic ones.

00:33:43.438 --> 00:33:46.399
But Kyrgyzstan's future is
taking shape out here too.

00:33:49.068 --> 00:33:52.613
That's thanks in large part to
this extremely cheerful man,

00:33:55.450 --> 00:33:57.910
Tolondu Toichubaev.

00:34:02.415 --> 00:34:05.043
Tolondu made a fortune
in the biotech business

00:34:05.043 --> 00:34:06.586
all over the world.

00:34:06.586 --> 00:34:08.296
Then came back to Kyrgyzstan,

00:34:08.296 --> 00:34:12.091
in hopes of making his homeland
more prosperous and free.

00:34:13.051 --> 00:34:15.094
He's set out to build a series of schools

00:34:15.094 --> 00:34:16.262
throughout the country.

00:34:17.138 --> 00:34:20.224
The first one came to life
here, where he grew up.

00:34:20.224 --> 00:34:22.101
We're going to your school?

00:34:22.101 --> 00:34:24.479
- Yeah. It's a first school.

00:34:24.479 --> 00:34:27.857
We built it in 10 years ago, 11 years ago.

00:34:27.857 --> 00:34:28.983
First school.

00:34:28.983 --> 00:34:32.195
- And how close is it to
where you went to school?

00:34:32.195 --> 00:34:34.572
- I grew up on the
village, on this village.

00:34:34.572 --> 00:34:37.909
But I always been
walking from this village

00:34:37.909 --> 00:34:38.993
until that village.

00:34:38.993 --> 00:34:39.827
- Okay.

00:34:39.827 --> 00:34:40.661
- Two miles every day.

00:34:40.661 --> 00:34:42.205
- You walked two miles in the snow?

00:34:42.205 --> 00:34:44.082
- In the snow. It was..

00:34:45.166 --> 00:34:46.000
- Classic.

00:34:46.000 --> 00:34:49.170
- Yeah. The classical story.

00:34:52.465 --> 00:34:53.299
- Here we go.

00:34:57.261 --> 00:34:58.429
Oh wow, it's huge!

00:34:58.429 --> 00:35:00.139
- It's huge. It's huge.

00:35:01.724 --> 00:35:03.810
- It's music time, it looks like

00:35:07.063 --> 00:35:08.898
- It's always the best time, music time.

00:35:08.898 --> 00:35:10.066
- Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:35:11.692 --> 00:35:14.237
This one, it's all local flora and fauna.

00:35:14.237 --> 00:35:15.404
- Okay.

00:35:15.404 --> 00:35:16.739
- Which we have in Kyrgyzstan.
- Okay.

00:35:18.407 --> 00:35:20.868
Tolondu wants his schools
to foster creative

00:35:20.868 --> 00:35:22.495
and critical thinking,

00:35:22.495 --> 00:35:25.456
in contrast to what he sees
as the more rote instruction

00:35:25.456 --> 00:35:26.666
at government schools.

00:35:27.708 --> 00:35:30.628
He's built 15 schools across Kyrgyzstan,

00:35:30.628 --> 00:35:33.506
and plans to build 15 more.

00:35:33.506 --> 00:35:36.134
He's funded much of
this operation to date.

00:35:36.134 --> 00:35:38.761
But hopes the schools will
soon be self-sustaining.

00:35:42.932 --> 00:35:45.226
Tell me about your whole vision

00:35:45.226 --> 00:35:47.436
of how you started the schools

00:35:47.436 --> 00:35:48.813
and where you want to take it?

00:35:48.813 --> 00:35:52.191
- It's a simple what for you need money.

00:35:52.191 --> 00:35:53.568
Money should work for something.

00:35:53.568 --> 00:35:55.403
So that's why I had the first idea

00:35:55.403 --> 00:35:58.156
that I have to have 10 schools.

00:35:58.156 --> 00:36:02.535
I will have a $1 million
for one school every year.

00:36:02.535 --> 00:36:03.452
And then I said,

00:36:03.452 --> 00:36:06.164
"Okay, I now I have to find a business

00:36:06.164 --> 00:36:08.166
which will generate enough money

00:36:08.166 --> 00:36:09.709
to build the schools for me."

00:36:16.132 --> 00:36:17.300
- Tolondu was a teenager

00:36:17.300 --> 00:36:20.428
when the Soviet Union collapsed in 1991,

00:36:20.428 --> 00:36:23.222
leaving Kyrgyzstan's economy in shambles.

00:36:24.348 --> 00:36:26.809
He was able to get a
medical degree in Bishkek,

00:36:26.809 --> 00:36:28.769
then took a job in the government.

00:36:28.769 --> 00:36:31.772
But quickly found himself at a dead end.

00:36:31.772 --> 00:36:35.401
- For me, it was on front
of mind, it's two way.

00:36:35.401 --> 00:36:40.031
Either I have to corrupted.
Either I have to poor.

00:36:40.031 --> 00:36:42.325
- So you thought if you
stay at the government,

00:36:42.325 --> 00:36:44.827
and you just do your
job and you do it well,

00:36:44.827 --> 00:36:46.037
you're going to be poor.

00:36:46.037 --> 00:36:46.871
'Cause I think it's

00:36:46.871 --> 00:36:49.415
- Yeah, because my
salary was, at that time,

00:36:49.415 --> 00:36:51.792
that time it was 50 bucks a month.

00:36:51.792 --> 00:36:53.920
So I decided to start my business.

00:36:53.920 --> 00:36:56.214
But while I am from the village

00:36:56.214 --> 00:36:58.424
and I don't have relatives
who can help for me.

00:36:58.424 --> 00:37:01.135
And so I decided that what I can do,

00:37:01.135 --> 00:37:02.803
I can sell my brain only.

00:37:02.803 --> 00:37:05.389
And so I decided to build
a consulting company.

00:37:08.476 --> 00:37:11.229
- He started a medical
diagnostic business.

00:37:11.229 --> 00:37:15.608
And who wouldn't buy medical
diagnostics from this man?

00:37:15.608 --> 00:37:18.945
Tell me everywhere you
have a diagnostic lab now.

00:37:18.945 --> 00:37:21.697
It's Kazakhstan, Kyrgyzstan, Ukraine.

00:37:21.697 --> 00:37:24.325
- Yeah, Ukraine, Russia.

00:37:24.325 --> 00:37:26.661
Before, was in China, in Vietnam.

00:37:26.661 --> 00:37:29.330
- This has turned into a massive business.

00:37:29.330 --> 00:37:31.290
- Yes, and kind of enough
to build the schools.

00:37:31.290 --> 00:37:32.375
- [Ashlee] Yeah. Yeah.

00:37:36.337 --> 00:37:39.090
You want all these kids
to be able to participate

00:37:39.090 --> 00:37:40.925
in the global economy.

00:37:40.925 --> 00:37:44.929
- Absolutely. So that's why
it's 80% of our students,

00:37:44.929 --> 00:37:48.391
out of our schools entering
the best universities in US

00:37:48.391 --> 00:37:49.392
and Europe.

00:37:49.392 --> 00:37:51.644
- That doesn't bother you
that they're going overseas,

00:37:51.644 --> 00:37:52.937
you feel like?

00:37:52.937 --> 00:37:55.648
- I believe that some of
them, they will stay there,

00:37:55.648 --> 00:37:57.525
in US or Europe.

00:37:57.525 --> 00:37:59.902
But I believe some of
them, they will come back.

00:37:59.902 --> 00:38:02.446
And I believe that critical
mass of that people

00:38:02.446 --> 00:38:03.447
will change country.

00:38:06.742 --> 00:38:09.328
Hi guys. How are you doing?

00:38:09.328 --> 00:38:12.123
We cool guys?

00:38:12.123 --> 00:38:13.291
Would be nice just to,

00:38:13.291 --> 00:38:16.877
can we join for you for
discussion, for your debate?

00:38:16.877 --> 00:38:17.753
- Yes.
- Yeah.

00:38:17.753 --> 00:38:18.713
- Good, good.

00:38:18.713 --> 00:38:19.797
- Here is this...

00:38:19.797 --> 00:38:21.966
- We happened to visit Tolondu's school

00:38:21.966 --> 00:38:24.051
on a very special day.

00:38:24.051 --> 00:38:25.386
We've got a debate going on.

00:38:25.386 --> 00:38:27.930
It's 10th and 11th
grade students, I think.

00:38:27.930 --> 00:38:28.764
- Yeah.

00:38:28.764 --> 00:38:31.225
- And it's kind of, should
art be taught in schools?

00:38:31.225 --> 00:38:32.852
And we've got pro arts

00:38:32.852 --> 00:38:35.688
and we've got the cruel anti-art people.

00:38:40.192 --> 00:38:45.197
- Art brings harmony and
beauty into a person's life.

00:38:46.240 --> 00:38:49.368
- Science given the
students the opportunity

00:38:49.368 --> 00:38:52.913
to better understand
how and why things work.

00:38:52.913 --> 00:38:56.042
- I sat back and watched
the free thinking fly.

00:38:56.042 --> 00:39:00.588
- Learning to read music
helped to develop our brain

00:39:00.588 --> 00:39:03.924
and to increase our mental capacity.

00:39:03.924 --> 00:39:04.759
- It was kind of like a scene

00:39:04.759 --> 00:39:06.469
from "Dead Poets Society."

00:39:06.469 --> 00:39:09.263
If the students were also
being stunningly eloquent

00:39:09.263 --> 00:39:10.639
in their third language.

00:39:10.639 --> 00:39:12.308
- Providing the arts adequately

00:39:12.308 --> 00:39:15.353
as part of the curriculum cost extra money

00:39:15.353 --> 00:39:17.646
in teacher time and resources.

00:39:17.646 --> 00:39:21.108
- I urge you to support our position

00:39:21.108 --> 00:39:24.278
that art isn't essential
to be in the school to

00:39:28.324 --> 00:39:30.493
- I think it's would be
nice just to understand,

00:39:30.493 --> 00:39:32.620
do you Opposition or in
Parliament, actually?

00:39:32.620 --> 00:39:35.664
- Well, I thought both
sides made good arguments.

00:39:35.664 --> 00:39:39.168
I am a reporter and a TV maker

00:39:39.168 --> 00:39:42.046
who covers science and technology,

00:39:42.046 --> 00:39:45.174
but I'm making art about
science and technology.

00:39:45.174 --> 00:39:47.385
So I think we can't have them split.

00:39:47.385 --> 00:39:48.886
We have to have both together.

00:39:50.721 --> 00:39:54.225
- From my position, I
take the simple things.

00:39:54.225 --> 00:39:56.018
Do you know what this mean, guys?

00:39:56.018 --> 00:39:59.480
I believe that 75% of this is art.

00:39:59.480 --> 00:40:02.817
And mostly I think it's
without linkage between art

00:40:02.817 --> 00:40:05.986
and the technology,

00:40:05.986 --> 00:40:09.782
you couldn't create new,
exciting things in the world.

00:40:22.002 --> 00:40:23.921
- I'm trying to know
how optimistic to feel

00:40:23.921 --> 00:40:25.506
about Kyrgyzstan.

00:40:25.506 --> 00:40:26.507
It still seems like you have some

00:40:26.507 --> 00:40:28.008
of these fundamental problems,

00:40:28.008 --> 00:40:30.136
that there's limited natural resources.

00:40:30.136 --> 00:40:33.722
There's people like you that
are trying to educate students

00:40:33.722 --> 00:40:35.933
and there's people pushing in technology.

00:40:35.933 --> 00:40:38.144
But it's going to be a long journey.

00:40:39.812 --> 00:40:41.063
- Yeah, it can be a long journey.

00:40:41.063 --> 00:40:41.897
- Yeah.

00:40:41.897 --> 00:40:45.443
- But we are lucky that we
don't have mineral resources.

00:40:45.443 --> 00:40:47.695
We are lucky that we don't have oils.

00:40:47.695 --> 00:40:48.529
- Okay.

00:40:48.529 --> 00:40:52.116
- And all of situations
pushing us to think more,

00:40:52.116 --> 00:40:54.410
what we can do, what we can achieve.

00:40:54.410 --> 00:40:59.206
Because I see some kind
of countries who has,

00:40:59.206 --> 00:41:00.749
for example, oil.

00:41:00.749 --> 00:41:03.294
And as they becoming lazy.

00:41:03.294 --> 00:41:07.381
Because it's easy, take
oil and sell it, it's easy.

00:41:07.381 --> 00:41:11.135
If you depends only on the human being,

00:41:11.135 --> 00:41:13.554
then it's a question, then
they're starting to move.

00:41:21.645 --> 00:41:22.605
- Back in Bishkek,

00:41:22.605 --> 00:41:25.774
there are signs that many things
in Kyrgyzstan are changing.

00:41:25.774 --> 00:41:27.234
And changing for the better.

00:41:33.741 --> 00:41:36.410
This building may not look
like much from the outside.

00:41:37.286 --> 00:41:39.330
In fact, it looks pretty terrible.

00:41:41.624 --> 00:41:43.042
But on the inside,

00:41:43.042 --> 00:41:46.629
there's a group of young
women aiming for the stars.

00:41:46.629 --> 00:41:48.756
Or at least, for low Earth orbit.

00:41:50.174 --> 00:41:54.053
Kyzzhibek here is the Director
of the Kyrgyz Space Program,

00:41:54.053 --> 00:41:57.431
aka, the Satellite
Building School for Girls.

00:42:01.185 --> 00:42:03.979
The goal is to make
Kyrgyzstan's first satellite

00:42:03.979 --> 00:42:06.524
and fly it into space.

00:42:06.524 --> 00:42:09.985
- Unfortunately, with
the collapse of the USSR,

00:42:09.985 --> 00:42:14.740
we did not have any space
projects or space agencies.

00:42:14.740 --> 00:42:19.578
So that's what we are trying
to do, to revive this industry.

00:42:19.578 --> 00:42:23.123
And we are also doing
it with a team of girls,

00:42:23.123 --> 00:42:26.126
because we really want
to convey our message

00:42:26.126 --> 00:42:29.213
that we need more girls
in STEM, in technology.

00:42:32.841 --> 00:42:36.887
So this is the first
satellite in our country

00:42:36.887 --> 00:42:38.264
that we brought here.

00:42:40.808 --> 00:42:42.101
- The big reveal.

00:42:42.977 --> 00:42:46.438
- This is a prototype of a CubeSat.

00:42:46.438 --> 00:42:48.482
Of course you don't
want this to go to space

00:42:48.482 --> 00:42:49.817
because it's not really protected

00:42:49.817 --> 00:42:54.822
and doesn't use the materials
that are able to endure space.

00:42:55.114 --> 00:42:58.367
But it's a great model to learn with

00:42:58.367 --> 00:43:01.370
- Yeah. And if I can
nerd out for a second,

00:43:01.370 --> 00:43:03.330
'cause I'm a satellite nerd.

00:43:03.330 --> 00:43:06.375
Not too long ago, if you
wanted to build a satellite,

00:43:06.375 --> 00:43:08.002
you had to do everything from scratch.

00:43:08.002 --> 00:43:09.086
It was very expensive.

00:43:09.086 --> 00:43:11.755
It was like reinventing
the wheel every time.

00:43:13.841 --> 00:43:17.094
And then a couple universities
in the United States

00:43:17.094 --> 00:43:18.804
came up with this CubeSat idea

00:43:18.804 --> 00:43:20.848
to sort of standardize the shape,

00:43:20.848 --> 00:43:22.308
some of the components you could put in.

00:43:22.308 --> 00:43:24.768
And so this is just a really cool thing,

00:43:24.768 --> 00:43:27.021
where it gives you this huge headstart.

00:43:27.021 --> 00:43:28.856
It pulls the price down.

00:43:28.856 --> 00:43:31.400
- Yes, that's totally true.

00:43:31.400 --> 00:43:32.568
Even 30 years ago,

00:43:32.568 --> 00:43:35.446
we would not have imagined
we would be able to do that.

00:43:35.446 --> 00:43:38.032
And now you can see space is successful.

00:43:38.032 --> 00:43:39.199
It's open to everyone.

00:43:39.199 --> 00:43:40.034
- Yeah.
- That's great.

00:43:40.034 --> 00:43:42.620
- We totally take a
nation state to-do list.

00:43:42.620 --> 00:43:43.662
- Yeah, so basically,

00:43:43.662 --> 00:43:46.040
the idea was to understand

00:43:46.040 --> 00:43:49.793
what components does the
CubeSat consist from?

00:43:49.793 --> 00:43:51.712
How they communicate with each other,

00:43:51.712 --> 00:43:53.839
how they work with each other,

00:43:53.839 --> 00:43:58.093
and try to connect them to our own sensor.

00:43:58.093 --> 00:43:59.261
When people ask us,

00:43:59.261 --> 00:44:01.764
"What is your satellite
going to do in space?"

00:44:01.764 --> 00:44:03.432
- Yeah.
- We say,

00:44:03.432 --> 00:44:06.644
"It doesn't matter what
it is doing in space.

00:44:06.644 --> 00:44:09.271
It's going to be the first
satellite of our country."

00:44:09.271 --> 00:44:10.397
- Yeah.
- In space.

00:44:10.397 --> 00:44:13.233
We will have some space heritage.

00:44:13.233 --> 00:44:14.818
We'll have knowledge, expertise,

00:44:14.818 --> 00:44:19.531
that we can transfer to an
educational institution,

00:44:19.531 --> 00:44:23.452
to promote space and
to revive the industry.

00:44:29.291 --> 00:44:30.417
- If ever there's been a time

00:44:30.417 --> 00:44:33.504
when this plan could work, it's right now.

00:44:33.504 --> 00:44:36.382
With tiny starter satellites
available for purchase

00:44:36.382 --> 00:44:37.800
and commercial rocket companies

00:44:37.800 --> 00:44:40.969
selling ever more affordable
flights into orbit.

00:44:40.969 --> 00:44:42.805
There's never been an easier time

00:44:42.805 --> 00:44:44.807
to get your gadget off-planet.

00:44:47.768 --> 00:44:50.104
That doesn't mean it's trivial.

00:44:50.104 --> 00:44:52.564
Kyzzhibek still needs to
raise a few hundred grand

00:44:52.564 --> 00:44:55.943
to get a spot on a SpaceX
or a Rocket Lab launch.

00:44:55.943 --> 00:44:57.903
And it's always a dicey proposition,

00:44:57.903 --> 00:44:59.655
whether these things will actually work,

00:44:59.655 --> 00:45:01.865
once they're exposed to the void.

00:45:01.865 --> 00:45:04.493
So you need some money and you
still have some work to do.

00:45:04.493 --> 00:45:08.914
I mean, do you have a goal
date to fly the satellite?

00:45:08.914 --> 00:45:11.333
- Oh, that's the toughest question.

00:45:11.333 --> 00:45:14.086
I tell people that if I ever paid a dollar

00:45:14.086 --> 00:45:17.423
each time a person asks me this question.

00:45:17.423 --> 00:45:19.675
- We have to embrace
what people do in space,

00:45:19.675 --> 00:45:21.176
which is you just announce something,

00:45:21.176 --> 00:45:22.219
and they're just really late.

00:45:22.219 --> 00:45:23.387
So that's what everybody does.

00:45:23.387 --> 00:45:25.806
That's the, it's the beauty of aerospace.

00:45:25.806 --> 00:45:27.391
- Yes, yes, I know.

00:45:34.523 --> 00:45:35.691
- If the team succeeds,

00:45:35.691 --> 00:45:38.110
they'll give birth to a
Kyrgyz space industry.

00:45:39.027 --> 00:45:40.112
Even more important,

00:45:40.112 --> 00:45:43.449
they'll send an undeniable
message to the haters and losers

00:45:43.449 --> 00:45:45.284
who don't think women have a place

00:45:45.284 --> 00:45:47.411
in Kyrgyzstan's tech future.

00:45:47.411 --> 00:45:52.416
- They see, I think women
of my kind, like a danger.

00:45:53.709 --> 00:45:54.960
They're scared.
- Yeah.

00:45:54.960 --> 00:45:56.837
And they say, you have,

00:45:56.837 --> 00:45:59.548
I'm not sure that it's
supposed to be on the camera.

00:45:59.548 --> 00:46:00.924
Like you have balls.

00:46:00.924 --> 00:46:03.469
Why do you compare me to a man?

00:46:03.469 --> 00:46:06.680
Why am I supposed to have
balls? I don't like this trend.

00:46:06.680 --> 00:46:07.514
- Yeah.

00:46:07.514 --> 00:46:10.309
- If you are a cool woman,
you're compared to a man.

00:46:10.309 --> 00:46:13.270
Therefore, I think, for us as role models,

00:46:13.270 --> 00:46:16.690
it's not only important to
become role models in STEM,

00:46:16.690 --> 00:46:18.650
but also in personal life.

00:46:18.650 --> 00:46:21.653
Saying that, "I'm a strong
woman, I can do everything."

00:46:21.653 --> 00:46:26.241
And that's why my personal
life and my career,

00:46:26.241 --> 00:46:27.409
I can excel in them.

00:46:27.409 --> 00:46:30.078
They can both work out together.

00:46:35.042 --> 00:46:39.713
- I didn't have a lot of
expectations coming here.

00:46:39.713 --> 00:46:43.675
But even if you just spend
a week in Kyrgyzstan,

00:46:43.675 --> 00:46:46.762
there's some things that
become totally obvious.

00:46:51.016 --> 00:46:52.768
It's a beautiful country.

00:46:55.020 --> 00:46:58.440
The people must be among the
most generous in the world.

00:47:01.193 --> 00:47:05.239
And it's a country that's
fragile, in a delicate state,

00:47:05.239 --> 00:47:08.033
but one where there's a
lot of hope for the future.

00:47:08.992 --> 00:47:11.370
And anything could happen.

